COVERAGE CAVEAT (stated, not hidden): SubscriptionStoreAuthTest calls the controller
directly -- app(SubscriptionController::class)->business_plan($this->req(...)) --
bypassing the HTTP kernel and route middleware. QA verified live that the route group
`['prefix'=>'vendor','namespace'=>'Vendor']` in routes/api/v1/api.php carries NO
middleware, so the direct call is behaviourally equivalent; and the live HTTP results
for the operating-store paths (403 with bearer / 401 without / 404 unknown store)
match the unit expectations exactly. The harness is therefore validated against live
HTTP for every path QA could reach.

FIXTURE-QUALITY NOTE: the suite's CUSTOMER_TOKEN is the opaque literal
'customer-passport-token-xyz', not a real Passport JWT. QA's live run confirms that is
faithful: a REAL customer Passport JWT (probe A) and a garbage string (probe C) both
returned an identical 403, because authorizeStore only does a string lookup against
vendors.auth_token / vendor_employees.auth_token and never parses the token.
The suite also guards this with assertResolvesToNoVendor() -- a positive fixture-premise
assertion, so the "non-vendor" claim is not assumed.
